    },
    "prompt": "Find the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}+3x$ at the point $(-1,-2).$\n\n${m}^{'}=$\n{{field-1}}",
    "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+3x)=2x+3\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(-1,-2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(-1)=2(-1)+3=-2+3=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{'}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{'} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "source_omissions": null,
