    "prompt": "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}-2{x}^{2}$ at the point $(1,0).$",
    "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}-2{x}^{2}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}-2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3/2}-2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{1/2}-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1)=3(1)^{1/2}-4(1)=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=1.\n$$",
