    "prompt": "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}+7x$ at the point $(1,9).$",
    "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}+7x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}+7x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-5}+7x) \\\\  & =-10{x}^{-6}+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1)=-10(1)^{-6}+7=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$",
